WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.510
More than 35 years after the release of its
low-budget, outrageous first installment in

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:07.259
1981, Evil Dead is still alive.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.580
With four movies and a TV series under its
banner, the franchise just can't beaten.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:20.460
Here's how this crazy, campy, chainsaw-happy
movie about one guy taking on demons in a

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.660
remote forest cabin went from the backwoods
of Michigan to the delirious nightmares of

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.840
horror fans worldwide.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.140
Three schmoes

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.151
The film was willed into existence by one
essential trio: actor Bruce Campbell, director

00:00:32.151 --> 00:00:36.370
Sam Raimi, and producer Robert Tapert, though
none of the three had those titles when they

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.050
came together to start their project.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.640
They were instead, in the words of Bruce Campbell,
"three schmoes in search of a clue."

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.460
Unbeknownst to all of them, they were destined
for a long future together, and it all started

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.140
with a series of low-budget Super 8 films
they made together in college.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.550
After cobbling together a catalog of shorts,
the three started thinking about producing

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:58.130
a full-fledged feature, and in 1979, Raimi
came up with a story about some kids who accidentally

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:01.210
summon evil spirits from a sacred burial ground.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.370
Within the Woods

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:06.060
To make their horror project, which they wanted
to name The Book of the Dead, the trio first

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.780
finished a short film as a proof of concept,
calling it "Within the Woods."

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.909
With a budget of $1,600, they shot the half-hour
movie over the course of a three-day weekend.

00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:24.570
Then, they set out to find investors to make
the full-length film.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.020
The only problem was that nobody knew who
they were.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.840
To make up for their lack of credibility,
they staged what Campbell estimated were hundreds

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.700
of screenings of "Within the Woods," picking
up a dollar here and there.

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:47.719
After months of work the trio came up with
$85,000 — not nearly enough to finish the

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:49.240
movie, but enough to get started.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.319
Pain and celluloid

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:55.329
Knowing money was tight, the trio was merciless
when it came to keeping costs contained.

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:58.520
To get a shot of the camera breaking through
a window, they just shoved the camera through

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.310
a real window, while another guy smashed the
window with a two-by-four to break the glass

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.609
before the lens touched it.

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:04.609
According to Campbell:

00:02:04.609 --> 00:02:07.639
"When all else failed, we just taped the damn
camera to Sam's hand.

00:02:07.639 --> 00:02:12.370
The opening shot of Evil Dead consisted of
me pushing Sam in a rubber raft across a swamp

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:16.860
while he leaned out, skimming the camera across
the water and swooping over decaying branches."

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.480
That wasn't the only time the actors had to
suffer indignities for the film — not by

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:21.480
a long shot.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.680
One actress lost her eyelashes while making
a plaster mold of her face.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.730
Others were mercilessly scratched up by branches
in the woods.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:31.120
Mosquitoes swarmed to the Karo corn syrup
that the crew used for blood.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.110
At one point, Campbell sprained his ankle
on set, and Raimi took to whacking the busted

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:39.580
ankle with a stick before takes to get a better
performance out of him.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:40.799
Wild success

00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:44.480
An "overnight success" is rarely as simple
as it sounds, but that's exactly what the

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:45.980
movie's producers had their hands on.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.049
The finished film became a sensation thanks
in large part to some well-timed support from

00:02:50.049 --> 00:02:53.989
horror author Stephen King, who wrote a review
for the movie in Twilight Zone magazine.

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:57.700
There, he called it "the most ferociously
original horror film of the year."

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.670
The filmmakers eagerly capitalized on this,
getting permission to use the quote on every

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.099
advertisement they could.

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:07.099
Raimi can't overstate how important this was
for their success, saying that King's endorsement

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:09.939
"opened the doorway for the film to be seen."

00:03:09.939 --> 00:03:11.209
Dead by Dawn

00:03:11.209 --> 00:03:16.219
Sam Raimi's next film was 1985's Crimewave,
a goofy crime comedy that completely failed

00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:17.920
to make a dent at the box office.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.299
After that failure, the trio decided to return
to their roots and make a sequel to their

00:03:21.299 --> 00:03:24.200
first feature: Evil Dead II: Dead by Dawn.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.900
A bigger budget and the backing of a studio
didn't alter the filmmakers' DIY methods.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.790
Most effects were made with the same low-budget
approach, and there was no shortage of suffering

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.209
on the set this time around, either — though
a lot of the burden fell on one unfortunate

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:38.629
performer: the director's brother, Ted Raimi.

00:03:38.629 --> 00:03:42.810
In a foam body suit, Ted portrayed the possessed
old lady, and he was so hot inside all the

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.430
foam and latex that he was constantly sweating.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:49.299
As Bruce Campbell pointed out, in one scene
you can see a stream of sweat pouring out

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:50.439
of the suit.

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:51.680
Army of Darkness

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.970
In the five years between Evil Dead II and
its sequel, Sam Raimi filmed his first major

00:03:55.970 --> 00:04:00.640
studio movie, Darkman, which was successful
enough to get him enough funding from Universal

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.290
to bring life to the third installment in
the franchise, Army of Darkness.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:08.120
This time, instead of sticking with the cabin-based
shenanigans of the first two movies, our hero

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:12.470
Ash travels back to medieval times to fight,
well, the Army of Darkness.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:16.750
Unfortunately, creative differences plagued
the movie's long road to a theatrical release.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:20.010
Thanks to a dispute between Universal and
one of the film's producers, the film sat

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.450
on the shelf for a year.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.320
Afterward, the studio decided to do a major
recut of the movie.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.860
According to editor Bob Murawski, "They didn't
really understand it, they thought it was

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.640
too long, and didn't respond to a lot of the
funnier stuff."

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.980
The ending in particular was a point of contention,
with the studio wanting something more optimistic.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.730
One intended ending had the series hero, Ash,
accidentally waking up in a post-apocalyptic

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.000
future, but it was axed for being too sad.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:52.820
This resulted in the filming of a new ending
sequence, set in the present-day S-Mart after

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.410
Ash makes it back to his own time and dispatches
one final demon.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:57.440
Which was still pretty awesome.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.540
Modern-day Ash

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:06.910
In 2013, all three of the original schmoes
— Campbell, Raimi, and Tapert — produced

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:10.410
the Evil Dead remake, with newcomer Fede Alvarez
directing.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.540
Although fans and critics alike were mostly
pleased with the remake, there was still one

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.880
thing everyone wanted: more Ash.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:20.170
As Sam Raimi described it: "[Fans] really
loved [the 2013 remake], and I loved it.

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:24.200
But afterwards, I still heard that, 'Now we
want your Evil Dead with Bruce' [...] I didn't

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.450
know if I had much to gain, but they were
so insistent we finally said, 'All right,

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:29.510
let's just do it.'"

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:33.710
Along with his brother Ivan, Raimi finally
began work on another, Ash-centric installment

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:37.080
of the franchise, eventually landing on the
idea to do a TV show.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.460
It was a natural fit, with the Evil Dead crew
having worked extensively in TV on the fantasy

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.460
show Hercules: The Legendary Journeys.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:45.610
The future

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:50.210
Since it debuted in 2015, Ash vs. Evil Dead
has been a hit with both audiences and critics.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:54.000
In a lot of ways, there's never been a better
time to be a fan of this series.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.410
So what's next for the franchise?

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.870
In the wake of Alvarez's remake, there was
some talk about doing a crossover movie that

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.910
would collide the two franchise universes,
bringing Ash into the orbit of the remake's

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:03.910
characters.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:07.310
Plans for such a project are apparently on
hold, although when asked about the crossover's

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:11.630
chances on Twitter in 2016, Campbell stated
that it's still "entirely possible."

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:15.811
And it's true — when it comes to this enduring
horror show, the sky's the limit.

00:06:15.811 --> 00:06:19.430
To think that such a little film, originating
out of the deep woods of middle America through

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:23.900
sheer force of will, would continue to have
such an impact is pretty remarkable — or,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:24.780
as Ash would put it…

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.160
Thanks for watching!

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.470
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.680
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

